Ordinary prop types are still checked.

  $ cat > input.re <<EOF
  > let element = <div className=42 dataFoo="x" />;
  > EOF
  $ dune build @mel
  File "input.re", line 1, characters 29-31:
  1 | let element = <div className=42 dataFoo="x" />;
                                   ^^
  Error: The constant 42 has type int but an expression was expected of type
           string
  [1]

Data attribute values must be strings.

  $ cat > input.re <<EOF
  > let element = <div dataFoo=42 />;
  > EOF
  $ dune build @mel
  File "input.re", line 1, characters 27-29:
  1 | let element = <div dataFoo=42 />;
                                 ^^
  Error: The constant 42 has type int but an expression was expected of type
           string
  [1]

Optional data attribute values are checked too.

  $ cat > input.re <<EOF
  > let element = <div dataFoo=?Some(42) />;
  > EOF
  $ dune build @mel
  File "input.re", line 1, characters 33-35:
  1 | let element = <div dataFoo=?Some(42) />;
                                       ^^
  Error: The constant 42 has type int but an expression was expected of type
           string
  [1]

Renamed ordinary props retain their original types.

  $ cat > input.re <<EOF
  > let element = <div open_="yes" dataFoo="x" />;
  > EOF
  $ dune build @mel
  File "input.re", line 1, characters 25-30:
  1 | let element = <div open_="yes" dataFoo="x" />;
                               ^^^^^
  Error: This constant has type string but an expression was expected of type
           bool
  [1]

Unknown ordinary props still fail. Only show the final diagnostic, since the
constructor's list of valid props is long.

  $ cat > input.re <<EOF
  > let element = <div clasName="typo" dataFoo="x" />;
  > EOF
  $ dune build @mel > error.log 2>&1
  [1]
  $ tail -n 1 error.log
  This argument cannot be applied with label ~clasName

Data attribute names must be unique, including optional arguments.

  $ cat > input.re <<EOF
  > let element = <div dataFoo="first" dataFoo="second" />;
  > EOF
  $ dune build @mel
  File "input.re", line 1, characters 43-51:
  1 | let element = <div dataFoo="first" dataFoo="second" />;
                                                 ^^^^^^^^
  Error: JSX: duplicate data attribute "data-foo"
  [1]

  $ cat > input.re <<EOF
  > let render = (~dataFoo=?, ()) => <div ?dataFoo dataFoo="second" />;
  > EOF
  $ dune build @mel
  File "input.re", line 1, characters 55-63:
  1 | let render = (~dataFoo=?, ()) => <div ?dataFoo dataFoo="second" />;
                                                             ^^^^^^^^
  Error: JSX: duplicate data attribute "data-foo"
  [1]

Shadowing string must not change the type of a data attribute.

  $ cat > input.re <<EOF
  > type string = int;
  > let element = <div dataFoo=42 />;
  > EOF
  $ dune build @mel
  File "input.re", line 2, characters 27-29:
  2 | let element = <div dataFoo=42 />;
                                 ^^
  Error: The constant 42 has type string but an expression was expected of type
           string
  [1]

Optional ordinary props, event handlers, and refs retain their types.

  $ cat > input.re <<EOF
  > let element = <div className=?Some(42) dataFoo="x" />;
  > EOF
  $ dune build @mel
  File "input.re", line 1, characters 35-37:
  1 | let element = <div className=?Some(42) dataFoo="x" />;
                                         ^^
  Error: The constant 42 has type int but an expression was expected of type
           string
  [1]

  $ cat > input.re <<EOF
  > let element = <div onClick={event => event + 1} dataFoo="x" />;
  > EOF
  $ dune build @mel
  File "input.re", line 1, characters 37-42:
  1 | let element = <div onClick={event => event + 1} dataFoo="x" />;
                                           ^^^^^
  Error: The value event has type
           React.Event.Mouse.t = React.Event.Mouse.tag React.Event.synthetic
         but an expression was expected of type int
  [1]

  $ cat > input.re <<EOF
  > let element = <div ref=42 dataFoo="x" />;
  > EOF
  $ dune build @mel
  File "input.re", line 1, characters 23-25:
  1 | let element = <div ref=42 dataFoo="x" />;
                             ^^
  Error: The constant 42 has type int but an expression was expected of type
           ReactDOM.domRef
  [1]
